    "concept": "Internet Protocol (IP) - Time-to-Live (TTL) Field",
    "markdown": "- **Role of the Time-to-Live (TTL) Field:**\n  - The IPv4 header includes an $8$-bit **Time-to-Live (TTL)** field (replaced by the *Hop Limit* field in IPv6).\n  - Its primary purpose is to **prevent datagrams from circulating indefinitely** in the network due to routing loops (which can arise during routing convergence or misconfiguration).\n  - Each intermediate router that forwards the IP datagram decrements the TTL field by at least $1$. If the TTL field reaches $0$ before the packet reaches its final destination, the router discards the packet and sends an ICMP *Time Exceeded* (Type 11, Code 0) message back to the source IP address.\n\n- **Distractor Analysis:**\n  - **Option A & Option B:** IP is an unreliable, best-effort datagram service. The TTL field does not represent wall-clock time for real-time delivery guarantees or discarding late-arriving packets.\n  - **Option D:** Queuing time at intermediate routers is managed by buffer management algorithms (such as Drop Tail or RED), not by the TTL hop counter.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option C (Prevent packets from looping indefinitely)**.",
